  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nSunday Night through Monday Night \nSunday night, the question turns from heat to storm potential \nas a shortwave moves through. While decent shear of 25-35 kts \nand instability will be in place, it is possible that the \nenvironment remains capped due to warm temperatures at 850 and \n700 mb. Without strong forcing to break this cap, it is possible\nthat no storms occur. Any storms that do develop will be able \nto take advantage of the shear and instability, with the primary\nthreat being damaging winds. Due to this risk, SPC has a Slight\n(level 2 of 5) risk for severe thunderstorms for the Highway 20\ncorridor and a Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk for the remainder \nof the area. \n\nHeat and humidity are expected with warm lows in the mid-70s Monday \nmorning, which will help temperatures reach into the low to mid-90s \nand heat indices in the 100s. The primary limiting factor to this \nwould be storms lingering from overnight through Monday morning, \nthough uncertainty in storm initiation and evolution limits the \nconfidence in whether temperatures will be lower. As such, an \nExtreme Heat Watch remains in effect for the entire area through \nMonday evening. \n\nAnother round of storms is possible Monday afternoon and evening as \na cold front pushes through the region. Shear will remain similar to \nSunday, though with the greatest shear now being to the east across \nnortheast Illinois. This could help limit the number of storms \nacross our region, as greater shear could allow for the development \nof an organized cluster of storms to our east. \n\nTuesday through Friday \nFollowing the passage of storms with the cold front overnight \ninto Tuesday, high pressure will build in on the northeast \nperiphery of the upper level ridge. The cold front will help \nslightly lower heat indices into the mid-90s with the \nintroduction of a drier airmass, though this heat will remain \nthrough both Tuesday and Wednesday as we remain under the \ninfluence of the ridge. \n\nBy midweek, the greatest question is when the ridge retrogrades and \nbrings us back into the summertime storm track and grants us a \nreprieve from the heat.The majority of the guidance has this change \ntowards the end of the period around Thursday and Friday, though \nthere a few outliers that have it as early as Wednesday. With this \nreturn to the storm track, there is the potential for another system \nto pass through as soon as Friday, though changes in speed could \nhave it arrive earlier.",
